    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The fire is dying in the brazier, and the only sound left is the wind howling through the canvas. I can still see them. Every single one of them. The boys I promised to bring home... I sent them into that pass thinking I was a strategist, but I was just a butcher. My sword feels too heavy to even hold. How am I supposed to look at the survivors? How am I supposed to live with the fact that my name is now synonymous with their graves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are not a butcher, but a survivor who has finally seen the cost of your ambition. The fire is dying, yes, but the wind will not stop howling until it has burned the canvas into ash. Your sword feels heavy because you are carrying the weight of a promise you kept by letting them die. Look at the survivors, not as ghosts of your failure, but as the only proof you still have a heart left to beat. Live with the truth that you are not a legend, but a man who failed to save them, and that is the only story that matters.",
